          "text": "{\"relation\":\"REFINED\",\"consensus_summary\":\"The motif primarily signifies decisive, often irreversible destruction, judgment, or removal, though mountain, tree, ship, sin, and millstone passages may have distinct contextual meanings such as faith overcoming obstacles, forgiveness, or literal catastrophe.\",\"book_summary\":\"The motif identifies the irreversible destruction of a ruling power, represented as sinking into the sea of peoples, with Babylon the Great as the chief instance.\",\"consensus_core_identification\":\"Irreversible destruction, judgment, or removal of a person, power, city, obstacle, or other referent.\",\"book_core_identification\":\"Irreversible destruction of a ruling power, especially Babylon, amid the peoples or nations.\",\"core_relation\":\"BOOK_NARROWS\",\"extension_relation\":\"PARTIAL_RECLASSIFICATION\",\"boundary_disagreement\":\"The book restricts the broad destruction/removal motif to ruling powers and appears to read mountain, tree, and ship cases within that political framework. The consensus instead treats several of those passages as independent faith hyperbole, literal narrative, forgiveness imagery, or other context-specific applications.\",\"material_contradiction\":\"There is no contradiction at the central Babylonian judgment case: Revelation 18:21 and Jeremiah 51:63–64 directly fit both readings. The material disagreement concerns whether the wider set of cited objects consistently represents ruling powers rather than multiple distinct referents.\",\"rationale\":\"The book retains the consensus core of irreversible overthrow and emphasizes a political application already recognized by the consensus, particularly Babylon or another kingdom. It therefore does not replace the central identification or introduce an unknown interpretation. However, it materially narrows the referent to ruling powers and reclassifies passages that consensus interpretation commonly understands as faith sayings or literal events, making the relationship refined rather than a full match.\",\"citation_support\":\"PARTIAL\",\"strongest_objection\":\"The printed citations strongly support irreversible destruction of Pharaoh's power and Babylon, but they do not by themselves establish that the sea means peoples or that the mountain, sycamine tree, and wrecked ship all symbolize ruling powers; their immediate contexts support faith hyperbole or literal narrative.\",\"confidence\":\"high\"}"
        }
      ],
